The Location C network is fully operational. All four workstations (PC11–PC14, 192.168.200.11–.14/24) are configured correctly with a default route pointing to router4's LAN-side interface (eth0, 192.168.200.1/24). Direct probes contradict every claim in the ticket: ping from each PC to 192.168.200.1 completes with 0% packet loss, and cross-site reachability to 172.16.10.4 (Location A), 10.16.1.1, and 192.168.100.1 also succeeds with normal RTTs. The L2 path through switch5/switch6 → switch4 → router4 is intact; all switch uplinks are carrier-up. Router4 itself is healthy: eth0 (Location C LAN), eth4 (link to router5, 192.168.15.x), and eth5 (link to router3, 172.25.1.x) are all in state UP with correct addresses. RIP v2 is running and has a full routing table — routes to all remote subnets are installed via both uplink neighbors (router5 and router3) with fresh timers and correct metrics. There are no ACLs, firewall rules, or policy routes on router4 that would selectively drop traffic. The root cause is that no fault exists: the reported outage is a false alarm and the network has been continuously healthy. No corrective action is required; helpdesk should re-verify at the user's end (workstation-level software firewall or VPN client blocking ICMP are the most likely source of the mistaken report).
